WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.920
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.090
In this section we want to look
at how we can theoretically

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.450
describe masses of neutrinos.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.670
There is not just
one way to do this

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.030
and the judge is still out
there which one is actually

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.290
realized in nature, or perhaps
both are realized in nature.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.130
So mass terms can be
constructed by introducing

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.440
so-called sterile neutrinos.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.080
The first way, and it's
shown here in the Lagrangian,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.760
is very familiar
to you where you

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.270
have a left-handed
component of a particle

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.830
coupling via the Higgs boson
to a right-handed component.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.470
Now, if we now have a
right-handed component

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:51.460
or right-handed neutrino,
that neutrino does not

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.140
interact with the
weak interaction

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.290
and doesn't interact with any
other interaction we know.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.910
Hence, this neutrino,
this right-handed particle

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.570
is a sterile neutrino.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:07.420
It's not interacting in
any of known ways other

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:13.820
than with the Higgs field with
the rest of the standard model.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:20.780
The second part, the second way
the mechanism is using Majorana

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.600
particles, particles which
are their own antiparticles,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.030
and we'll look in how
this is being implemented.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.090
So again, the first,
the Dirac term

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.330
is generated after
electroweak symmetry breaking

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.670
from Yukawa interactions.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.850
We have seen the very same
thing for our charged leptons.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:42.665
What we see here is that the
lepton number is conserved.

00:01:42.665 --> 00:01:44.330
Before and after
the interaction we

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:48.530
have the same number of leptons,
but the lepton flavor is not

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.260
conserved in this interaction.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.890
We can rewrite this.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.857
We identify the sterile neutrino
as the right-handed component

00:01:57.857 --> 00:01:58.440
of the spinor.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.580
I mentioned this
already, and we basically

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.280
couple the
weak-doublet components

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.194
as you would just
expect that to appear.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.720
The second term, the
Majorana mass term,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.720
is interesting as we
introduce another singlet

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.950
into the standard model.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.790
So this then can
appear as a bare mass

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.862
term with some consequences.

00:02:26.862 --> 00:02:28.320
So here what we
are trying to do is

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.700
we are involved two neutrinos,
right-handed fields.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.040
Those break the lepton number.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.330
So if those neutrinos
are realized in nature,

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.310
we have to observe lepton--

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.730
we should observe lepton
number-violating processes.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.610
And so the search for the
specific kind of neutrino

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:53.400
is through searching for lepton
number-violating processes.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.440
So we can rewrite this
part of the Lagrangian,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.780
this part of the math term
here using this matrix.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.630
Let's see how this unfolds.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:06.810
If the math term now is
much, much larger, or larger

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.590
than the electroweak
scale, you can

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.140
try to diagonalize
the master and it

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.550
leads to three neutrinos,
three light neutrinos,

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.760
three light neutrinos
you would expect,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.060
and one potentially--
one potential, or maybe

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.970
multiple potential
heavy neutrinos.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.560
If you then rewrite
the math term

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:31.020
you find for the light
ones, the term which goes

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.400
is 1 over the scale of
this [? known ?] neutrino.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.640
That is a nice motivation
for this kind of physics

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.180
as it automatically reduces
the amount of the neutrino

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.500
as we observe the math is
to be very small in nature.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.630
And then the mass of
the heavy neutrino

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.430
is proportional to the mass.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:55.230
This mechanism is called see-saw
because it automatically moves

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.490
the scales of those
two neutrinos,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.720
the heavy ones and the light one
apart so you happen to observe

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.300
the heavy one, maybe because
they're very, very heavy,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.420
and the light ones have light
mass because of this mechanism

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.110
of being proportional to
1 over the mass scale--

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:18.399
the mass eigenstate
of those neutrinos.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:23.440
However, if the mass scale of
those eigenvalues is much--

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.360
not higher than the
electroweak scale,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.880
the low energy spectrum contains
these additional light states.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.140
So you have not just the
three light neutrinos but you

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.050
have additional light scales--

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.050
states which mix with these
three light neutrinos.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.530
And that is kind of
an interesting area

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.620
to look for these particles
as they would lead

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.310
to small deviations in
observed electroweak

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.820
efficient properties,
and they might

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.420
yield to some interesting
decays in nuclear physics,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.130
and we'll come to
those specifics later.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:02.550
We have seen in this
lecture two different ways

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.630
to generate masses.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.390
One's to recover
interactions, same

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:10.050
as the interaction with
the Higgs field, and one,

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:15.110
we have the see-saw mechanism
introducing Majorana neutrinos.

